# =============================================================================
# BACKEND (Cloud Functions) environment template. Copy to `functions/.env`.
# =============================================================================
#
# CONTAINS REAL SECRETS ONCE FILLED IN (SMTP URL, API keys). All
# `functions/.env*` files are gitignored except this template.
#
# HOW THE FIREBASE CLI RESOLVES THESE (later wins):
#   1. .env
#   2. .env.<projectId>         e.g. .env.tailed-community-dev
#   3. .env.<alias>             MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with .env.<projectId> —
#                               the CLI errors if both exist. Use projectId.
#   4. .env.local               loaded ONLY when running the emulator suite
#
# Reserved key prefixes the CLI REJECTS: X_GOOGLE_, FIREBASE_, EXT_.
# That is why our vars are FB_* and why we cannot set FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST
# here — `firebase emulators:start` injects it automatically anyway, along with
# FUNCTIONS_EMULATOR=true, which lib/env.ts uses to detect local.
# PORT is also reserved.
# =============================================================================

# --- Which BACKING RESOURCES does this talk to? ---
# REQUIRED: one of emulator | dev | production. lib/env.ts THROWS if unset, by
# design — there is no safe default. Guessing `emulator` silently swallows all
# email (the bug this replaced); guessing `production` makes a misconfigured
# dev project send real mail with production-branded URLs.
# Omit it only under `firebase emulators:start`, which sets
# FUNCTIONS_EMULATOR=true and is therefore inferred as `emulator`.
#
# This is ONE OF TWO AXES. The other — WHERE the process runs (cloud /
# emulator / standalone) — is auto-detected and is what decides whether we
# start our own HTTP listener. There is no `local` value here on purpose:
# "local" is a runtime, not an environment. That conflation is what made "dev"
# ambiguous between "on my laptop" and "the deployed dev project".
#
#   setup           runtime      APP_ENV      listener  email    Firebase
#   local emulator  emulator     emulator     no        console  emulator
#   local-dev       standalone   dev          YES :3001 real     dev project
#   deployed-dev    cloud        dev          no        real     dev project
#   production      cloud        production   no        real     prod project
#
# This file (functions/.env) is the LOCAL-DEV config — a developer laptop
# pointed at the real dev project. The deployed-dev config is written by CI.
APP_ENV=dev

# --- Firebase project ---
# Must match the frontend's VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID for the same environment.
FB_PROJECT_ID=tailed-community-dev
FB_API_KEY=
FB_AUTH_DOMAIN=
FB_STORAGE_BUCKET=
FB_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
FB_APP_ID=

# --- Email ---
# The PRESENCE of this variable decides whether mail is really sent — not the
# environment name. Set it and we send; leave it empty and deliver() logs the
# rendered message to the console instead. That also means you can point it at
# a local catcher (Mailpit: smtp://localhost:1025) with no code change.
#
# REQUIRED when APP_ENV is dev or production: assertEnvValid() refuses to start
# without it there, so a real environment can never silently swallow email.
# A full nodemailer connection URL, e.g. smtps://user:pass@smtp.example.com:465
EMAIL_SERVER=
EMAIL_FROM=Tail'ed <no-reply@tailed.ca>

# --- URLs ---
# Three DISTINCT things. They are separate variables because they legitimately
# differ (a magic link may land on localhost while email images must load from
# a real hosted domain).

# FRONTEND_URL: public site origin. Used for email assets (the logo PNG must be
# fetchable by mail clients, so NEVER localhost) and job/community links.
FRONTEND_URL=https://tailed-community-dev.firebaseapp.com

# AUTH_CONTINUE_URL: origin the passwordless magic link lands on (/auth/callback).
# Its domain MUST be in the Firebase authorized-domains list or
# generateSignInWithEmailLink fails with auth/unauthorized-continue-uri.
# `localhost` is allowlisted at any port.
#
# Runtime-aware default: standalone (laptop) -> http://localhost:5174,
# otherwise FRONTEND_URL. That default is what lets local-dev and deployed-dev
# share APP_ENV=dev while landing links in the right place, so you usually do
# NOT need to set this explicitly.
AUTH_CONTINUE_URL=http://localhost:5174

# API_PUBLIC_URL: public origin of THIS API. Used for links that must work
# without a session (one-click unsubscribe), so it can never be the frontend.
#
# Runtime-aware default: standalone -> http://localhost:3001, otherwise derived
# as https://us-central1-<FB_PROJECT_ID>.cloudfunctions.net/app. Deriving is
# deliberate — the old hardcoded fallback was a literal `tailed-community-dev`
# URL, so PRODUCTION emails could carry unsubscribe links pointing at DEV.
# Only set this explicitly if you use a custom domain.
API_PUBLIC_URL=

# --- Third party ---
DEVPOST_API_KEY=

# --- Credentials ---
# firebase-admin uses Application Default Credentials. Deployed functions get
# them automatically. Running OUTSIDE the emulator (nodemon, the dry-run
# scripts) against a REAL project needs a service account with the
# `iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator` role — generateSignInWithEmailLink signs a
# blob and fails without it. Either run `gcloud auth application-default login`
# or set GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS to a key file path.
# The emulator needs none of this.
#
# `generateDocumentUrl` (routes/profile.ts) needs the exact same signBlob
# capability to mint v4 signed URLs for resume/cover-letter/grades documents.
# Without it, ADC user credentials have no private key ("Cannot sign data
# without `client_email`") or the runtime SA lacks the IAM role
# (signBlob PERMISSION_DENIED). It memoizes the failure after the first
# attempt (one warning, not one per request) rather than retrying every call.
# When signing is unavailable it falls back to a permanent, unauthenticated
# download-token URL for resume/cover letter only — grades/transcripts
# deliberately return no URL rather than ever produce one of those for a
# sensitive document. Same two remedies apply:
#   - local-dev (standalone, this file): set
#     GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_BASE64 (or a key file via
#     GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS), or impersonate a token-creator-capable
#     service account via `gcloud auth application-default login`.
#   - deployed (dev/production): grant the runtime service account
#     `roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator` on itself (self-binding), so it
#     can sign blobs as its own identity without a downloaded key.
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_BASE64=
